      },
      "text": "DISCUSSION",
      "html": null,
      "level": 1,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "text",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.5417361344537815,
        "y0": 0.7145688836104513,
        "x1": 0.9501394957983192,
        "y1": 0.830166270783848
      },
      "text": "Aseptic loosening of endoprostheses presents a major challenge at revision, especially in cement removal and loss of bone stock. Cementless THAs were developed to overcome some of these problems, but worse results have been reported in comparison with ‘traditional’ cemented endoprostheses. 2,3 In our experience cementless ﬁxation of the femoral component gives good middle-term results, but the acetabular component may produce problems. 4 The threaded ﬁxation of the acetabular component gives",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    },
    {
      "block_type": "text",
      "box": {
        "page": 0,
        "x0": 0.5411764705882353,
        "y0": 0.828978622327791,
        "x1": 0.9495798319327731,
        "y1": 0.9156769596199525
      },
      "text": "good primary stability with only a small variation due to differences in design. 11 The short-term results were encouraging, 12,13 but in more recent studies with a maximum follow-up of seven years high rates of failure have been reported. 4,7,14,15",
      "html": null,
      "level": null,
      "section_hierarchy": []
    }
